{"id":918,"date":"2026-03-07T14:05:28","date_gmt":"2026-03-07T14:05:28","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:10005\/?p=918"},"modified":"2026-03-08T06:28:34","modified_gmt":"2026-03-08T06:28:34","slug":"%e5%9c%a8%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90%e4%b8%ad%e5%88%a9%e7%94%a8%e6%81%b6%e6%84%8f%e5%8f%af%e9%87%8d%e6%9e%84%e6%99%ba%e8%83%bd%e8%a1%a8%e9%9d%a2%e7%aa%83%e5%8f%96","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e5%9c%a8%e7%89%a9%e7%90%86%e5%b1%82%e5%af%86%e9%92%a5%e7%94%9f%e6%88%90%e4%b8%ad%e5%88%a9%e7%94%a8%e6%81%b6%e6%84%8f%e5%8f%af%e9%87%8d%e6%9e%84%e6%99%ba%e8%83%bd%e8%a1%a8%e9%9d%a2%e7%aa%83%e5%8f%96\/","title":{"rendered":"Exploiting Malicious Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces to Steal Keys in Physical Layer Secret Key Generation"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Exploiting Malicious Reconfigurable Intelligent Surfaces to Steal Keys in Physical Layer Secret Key Generation<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">L. Hu, G. Li, A. Hu, and D. W. K. Ng<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>IEEE Wireless Communications Letters<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons lab-btn-group is-layout-flex wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/Exploiting_Malicious_RIS_for_Secret_Key_Acquisition_in_Physical-Layer_Key_Generation.pdf\">\ud83d\udcc4 PDF<\/a><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-fill\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https:\/\/ieeexplore.ieee.org\/document\/10310101\/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809 noreferrer noopener\">\ud83d\udd17 DOI Link<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface (RIS), with its ability to control the wireless propagation environment, can be leveraged to assist physical layer secret key generation based on wireless channels. However, when the RIS is controlled by an attacker, this capability also poses a serious security threat to the PKG system. To this end, this paper investigates a RIS leakage (RISL) attack, in which the attacker manipulates the RIS reflection coefficients to influence the keys generated by the legitimate parties. Specifically, in a block-fading environment, the attacker alternates the RIS reflection coefficients between all-zero and all-one within each block, causing the received signal strength at the legitimate parties to exhibit regular enhancement and attenuation over time, thereby producing predictable 0s and 1s in the quantized bits. To counter this attack, this paper proposes a defense scheme based on dynamic private pilots and proves that CDPP can significantly reduce the success rate of the RISL attack. Simulation results validate the effectiveness of the proposed attack and defense schemes, highlighting the importance of introducing artificial randomness in block-fading channels to ensure key security.<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Core Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Propose and analyze the RISL active attack<\/strong>: In a block-fading environment, the attacker controls the RIS to alternate the reflection coefficients between all-zero and all-one, causing the RSS to fluctuate regularly over time, which leads the quantized bit sequences of the legitimate parties to exhibit a fixed pattern (e.g., $\\{0,1,0,1,\\ldots\\}$), thereby enabling the attacker to almost fully predict the generated keys.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Derive a closed-form expression for the attack success rate<\/strong>: It is proven that the success rate of the RISL attack is determined by the power ratio of the end-to-end composite channel to the direct link, $|h_d + h_r|^2 \\,\/\\, |h_d|^2$\u2014when the RIS-induced link dominates, the attack success rate approaches 1 at high SNR; three strategies to enhance the attack effect are also analyzed (increasing the number of RIS elements, deploying the RIS near the legitimate end, and blocking the direct link).<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Propose the CDPP defense scheme and derive an upper bound on the success rate<\/strong>: Alice introduces artificial random coefficients $w(i)$ following a complex Gaussian distribution when transmitting private pilots, superimposing unpredictable randomness onto the channel gain, thereby disrupting the regular fluctuation of the RSS. It is theoretically proven that under CDPP protection, even when $|h_d + h_r|^2 \\,\/\\, |h_d|^2 \\to \\infty$, the upper bound of the attack success rate is only $0.6065^{L\/2}$, representing a fundamental improvement over the unprotected case where it approaches 1.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-b46d2053 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-03-15.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-03-15.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-03-15.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-03-15.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-920\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-07_22-03-15\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">This paper considers an RSS-based PKG model, where two single-antenna legitimate parties, Alice and Bob, generate symmetric keys by alternately probing the channel in TDD mode, while the attacker Eve is equipped with an RIS comprising <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>N passive reflection elements.<strong>RISL attack phase<\/strong>, Eve sets the reflection matrix to a zero matrix during odd-numbered probing rounds (only the direct link <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">h_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>hd\u200b is effective), and to an identity matrix during even-numbered rounds (the direct link and the RIS-induced link <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">h_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>hr\u200b are superimposed), causing the RSS in even-numbered rounds to be systematically stronger than in odd-numbered rounds, and the quantized bits of the legitimate parties to degenerate into a predictable alternating sequence <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mo>\u2026<\/mo><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">K_e = \\{0,1,\\ldots,0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\u3002<strong>CDPP defense phase<\/strong>, Alice multiplies the downlink pilot by a private random coefficient <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><mo>\u223c<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo separator=\"true\">,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">w(i) \\sim \\mathcal{CN}(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, and after Bob transmits a public uplink pilot, Alice multiplies the received signal <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>y<\/mi><mo>\u02c9<\/mo><\/mover><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">\\bar{y}_a(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>by <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">w(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , introducing unpredictable randomness into the equivalent channel gain <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">w(i)h_{ba}(i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , fundamentally disrupting the regularity of the RSS while preserving channel reciprocity between the legitimate parties.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46f43c70 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-3027cfb8 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-04-49.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-04-49.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-04-49.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/03\/PixPin_2026-03-07_22-04-49.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-921\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"PixPin_2026-03-07_22-04-49\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>The CDPP defense scheme suppresses the attack success rate from approaching 1 to a theoretical upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.6065<\/mn><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">6065^{L\/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> , while guaranteeing a minimum key rate of 0.147 bits\/channel use for CDPP<\/strong>: As shown in <strong>Fig. 4<\/strong> of the original paper, under the unprotected RISL attack, when <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">|h_d+h_r|^2\/|h_d|^2 &gt; 1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, the attack success rate monotonically approaches 1; whereas under CDPP protection,<math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">L=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">L=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>the success rates are strictly lower than <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6065<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">0.6065<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math> and <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.6065<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>\u2248<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">6065^2 \\approx 0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>, and as <math xmlns=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">\u2223<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application\/x-tex\">|h_d+h_r|^2\/|h_d|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>increases, it asymptotically converges to this upper bound, which is fully consistent with the theoretical derivation in Theorem 2.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Citation<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@article{hu2024risl,\n  author    = {Lei Hu and Guyue Li and Aiqun Hu and Derrick Wing Kwan Ng},\n  title     = {Exploiting Malicious {RIS} for Secret Key Acquisition in Physical-Layer Key Generation},\n  journal   = {IEEE Wireless Communications Letters},\n  volume    = {13},\n  number    = {2},\n  pages     = {417--421},\n  month     = feb,\n  year      = {2024},\n  doi       = {10.1109\/LWC.2023.3330809}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-content-justification-space-between is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-788841da wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Reconfigurable Intelligent Surface (RIS), with its ability to control the wireless propagation environment, can be utilized to assist wireless channel-based Physical Layer Secret Key Generation (PKG). However, when the RIS is controlled by an attacker, this capability also poses a serious security threat to the PKG system. To this end, this paper investigates a RIS leakage (RISL) attack, in which the attacker manipulates the RIS reflection coefficients to influence the keys generated by the legitimate parties.<\/p>","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":920,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"mypost","format":"standard","meta":{"_uag_custom_page_level_css":"","footnotes":""},"categories":[5],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-918","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-research"],"uagb_featured_image_src":{"full":["https:\/\/jiushihema.git